purpose: JavaScript. Lexing, parsing, and execution. Knows nothing about the DOM.

# Notably absent: dom. A JS engine that could reach into the DOM directly would
# make the binding layer optional, and the binding layer is where every
# same-origin check will live. Bindings arrive in M9 as a separate seam.
allow: util

public: Token.h Lexer.h Ast.h Parser.h Value.h Heap.h Interpreter.h Bytecode.h

extern:

max_tu_lines: 1300

budget: Token  header_lines=40 public_methods=8  members=9
budget: Lexer  header_lines=60 public_methods=12 members=3
# One tagged node rather than forty classes. The trade is written out at the
# top of Ast.h: a uniform tree to walk, at the cost of "which fields does this
# kind use" living in comments and in the parser rather than in types.
budget: Node     header_lines=30 public_methods=4  members=6
# One entry per production the grammar has, with the shape of each written next
# to it. It grows with the language and nothing else.
budget: NodeKind header_lines=70 public_methods=0  members=0
# The parser's shared state, split across two translation units: expressions in
# Parser.cpp and statements in ParserStatements.cpp.
budget: ParserImpl header_lines=90 public_methods=6 members=5
budget: Value       header_lines=60 public_methods=20 members=5
# A compiled pattern, as a value: the program behind it is shared and immutable,
# so copying a RegExp is a refcount rather than a recompile.
budget: RegExp         header_lines=40 public_methods=10 members=3
budget: RegExpFlags    header_lines=30 public_methods=2  members=7
budget: RegExpMatch    header_lines=30 public_methods=6  members=1
# --- The machine ------------------------------------------------------------
# One entry per thing the machine can do, with its effect on the operand stack
# written beside it. That comment is the contract the compiler and the VM have
# with each other, so the lines here are description rather than surface: the
# member and method counts are what would say this is growing behaviour, and an
# enum has neither. It grows with the language and nothing else.
# Grew by three for generators, which is the language growing: Yield and
# GeneratorEntry are the two halves of suspending a generator, and
# IterateDelegate is the step `yield*` needs and no other loop does -- it is
# the only one that pushes the iterator's *return* value rather than dropping
# it.
budget: Op              header_lines=195 public_methods=0  members=0
# One function's code and everything it names. Data: seven vectors, three
# scalars, and no behaviour. It grows when a chunk has to remember one more
# kind of thing, which has happened twice and should be argued for each time.
# Grew by three for slot resolution: the property keys a named access uses, the
# declaration records DeclareSlot reads, and the size of the function's own
# scope. All three are things the compiler worked out once so the machine does
# not have to work them out per execution, which is what this whole struct is.
# Grew by one for the flattened frame: whether this function's scopes live in
# the frame or on the heap. One bit, and it has to be here rather than derived
# at run time because the compiler resolved every name against the answer --
# a name in a flattened function is one hop count and a name in a scoped one is
# another, so the two disagreeing is not a slowdown, it is a wrong slot.
# Grew by one for async: whether calling this returns a promise and its body
# can suspend. On the code rather than on the function object, because it is a
# property of the *body* -- the machine reads it in PushFrame to make the
# promise and in Return to settle it, and both already have the code.
# Grew by one for generators, and it is the same question one bit over: what a
# call hands its caller. `is_async` says a promise, `is_generator` says an
# iterator, and the two are independent rather than a mode -- `async function*`
# is both, which is why they are not one enum. On the code for the same reason
# `is_async` is: PushFrame reads it to make the generator object, and the
# compiler reads it to decide whether the body gets an entry suspend.
budget: CompiledFunction header_lines=85 public_methods=0 members=17
# One call in progress. Every field is something a resumed frame would need, and
# that is the test for adding another -- this is the record `await` will have to
# be able to copy somewhere and put back.
# Grew by one for the flattened frame: where this call's bindings start on the
# locals stack. Passes the test above -- a resumed frame has to know where its
# own bindings are before it can read one.
# Grew by one for async: the promise the call returns. This is the field that
# makes the record above worth being a record -- a filed frame is found again
# through the reaction attached to this, and settling it is what a return or a
# throw out of an async call actually does.
# Grew by one for generators, and it passes the test above: a resumed frame has
# to know which generator it is the body of before a `yield` can record where it
# filed itself. The promise finds its frame through a reaction; a generator's
# frame finds its generator through this, which is the same relationship read
# from the other end.
budget: Frame           header_lines=56 public_methods=0  members=12
# The compiler. Large because the language is, and split across four translation
# units by what each compiles rather than by size: the emitter and the
# expressions in Compiler.cpp, statements and patterns in
# CompilerStatements.cpp, scopes and slot resolution in CompilerScopes.cpp, and
# how a function body becomes a chunk in CompilerFunctions.cpp. The last of
# those came out of the first when generators pushed it past the cap, and it
# was the right seam rather than the nearest one: `Function` and its two helpers
# are not expressions, which is what Compiler.cpp says it holds.
# The member count is what matters and it is small -- three tables, three depths
# and two flags, all of them state that exists only while one function is being
# emitted.
# Grew by three for slot resolution: the enclosing function's compiler, the
# scopes this one has open, and how many of those are not block scopes. The
# three together are the model of the run-time scope chain, and it has to be a
# model rather than a lookup because the whole point is to answer "how many
# scopes out, which slot" without running anything.
budget: Compiler        header_lines=175 public_methods=4  members=14
# One scope the compiler created, and therefore one whose layout it knows. A map
# and a count; if it ever needs a third field, what it is modelling has changed.
# Grew by one, and what it is modelling did change, which is what that line
# asked for. A scope used to be indexed from its own base because it was its
# own Environment; in a flattened function every scope shares the frame's one
# slice, so a block has to say where in it its own run of slots begins.
budget: CompiledScope   header_lines=28 public_methods=0  members=3

# The instruction list, its constant pools, and the flags the matcher reads off
# it. Data, with no behaviour of its own -- every member is one of those three.
budget: RegExpProgram  header_lines=30 public_methods=0  members=11
budget: CharSet        header_lines=20 public_methods=6  members=1
budget: MatchState     header_lines=15 public_methods=0  members=3
budget: MatchInstruction    header_lines=10 public_methods=0  members=4
# One entry per thing the matcher can do, with what each field means written
# next to it. It grows with the pattern syntax and nothing else.
budget: MatchOp             header_lines=45 public_methods=0  members=0
# Grew for classes: the home object `super` resolves against, the superclass,
# and the instance field initializers. All three are per-function state that
# has nowhere else to live -- a class *is* a function here, which is what keeps
# `new` from having to know which of the two forms it was handed.
# Grew by one for the frozen flag. It is a bool beside `marked_` rather than a
# property on the object, because a frozen object must refuse writes from a
# *builtin* too -- `arr.push` on a frozen array does not go through
# SetProperty -- and a property could be deleted by the page it is meant to
# constrain.
# Grew by one member and two methods for the compiled body. A function has
# either an AST body or a chunk and never both -- they are two spellings of the
# same thing, and which one a function got is what CallFunction dispatches on.
# The pointer is on the object rather than in a table beside the heap, which is
# where compiled patterns and the weak tables live, because this one is read on
# every call and a hash lookup per call is the one place that cost would show.
budget: Object      header_lines=160 public_methods=46 members=17
# Grew by one member and by the slot half of its surface. Bindings live in a
# vector now and the names index into it: compiled code resolved its names while
# compiling and indexes straight in, everything else still asks by name and pays
# a hash to reach the same slot. One store, two ways in -- which is why the
# values are in `slots_` alone and the map holds indices rather than bindings.
# Measured at 15.6ns per name operation before, against a 158ns loop iteration.
budget: Environment header_lines=60 public_methods=12 members=4
# A string or a symbol, and never both. Two members and no room for a third:
# if a third kind of key ever appears it should be argued for here.
budget: PropertyKey header_lines=40 public_methods=10 members=2
# One key, as Map and Set compare keys. Large for what it holds because the
# comparison and the hash are written out beside it rather than derived: this
# is SameValueZero, which is neither `===` nor Object.is, and the two places it
# differs are worth being able to read.
budget: ValueKey    header_lines=90 public_methods=4  members=6
budget: MapIndex    header_lines=20 public_methods=0  members=2
# Grew by one member and two methods for the compiled patterns behind RegExp
# objects. They are kept beside the object rather than in it: every JavaScript
# object would otherwise carry a pointer for a kind of object a page makes a
# handful of, and the collector is already what knows when an object dies, so
# the sweep that frees the object drops the entry.
# Grew again for the weak tables. They are here and not on the object for the
# reason the compiled patterns are, only more so: a weak entry survives exactly
# when its key does, and only the collector can answer that. The ephemeron pass
# in Collect is what makes it true -- marking a value can make another table's
# key live, so the mark runs to a fixpoint rather than once.
# Grew by one for the generator states, on exactly the terms the compiled
# patterns and the map indexes are here: native state belonging to one object,
# dropped by the sweep that frees it rather than by a second lifetime story
# that could disagree. One reason applies only here -- in the object it would
# be a property, and a page that wrote its own would have `next` put the same
# frame back twice, which is a use-after-free with a script behind it.
budget: Heap        header_lines=145 public_methods=24 members=9
# The tree-walking interpreter.
#
# The member count went *down* at the commit that raised these numbers, from
# nineteen to thirteen: the seven well-known values -- the five prototypes,
# Promise.prototype and the `Symbol.iterator` cell -- are one grouped member
# now rather than seven loose ones. That was the budget doing its job. The old
# shape kept the fields here and the root list in MaybeCollect, so adding one
# and forgetting the other was a use-after-free nothing would catch until a
# page allocated enough; `WellKnown::Roots()` is now the only list.
#
# The header is larger than before because that grouping is written out where
# it lives, along with the microtask record. Lines here are description; the
# member and method counts are what say whether this is becoming a god object,
# and both have room.
# The well-known values, grouped. One field per thing the language requires to
# exist and one `Roots()` that cannot disagree with the fields -- which is the
# whole reason it is a struct. It grows as the language does.
# Grew by one for %GeneratorPrototype%, which is the language growing: `next`,
# `throw`, `return` and the `Symbol.iterator` that hands back the generator
# itself. One object shared by every generator rather than one per generator
# function, which is the one place a page could tell and is not a place a page
# looks.
budget: WellKnown   header_lines=52 public_methods=2  members=9
# Grew by one member for the machine: VmState groups the value stack, the frame
# stack, the block scopes, the open iteration cursors and the compiled programs.
# One member rather than five, for the reason WellKnown above is one rather than
# seven -- every field in it is a GC root, and a root list living apart from the
# fields is a use-after-free waiting for a page to allocate enough. GatherVmRoots
# sits next to the struct and walks all of it.
# Grew by one member for the calls that are waiting: Suspensions groups the
# filed frames and the counter that names them, for the reason WellKnown and
# VmState are each one member rather than several -- every value in there is a
# GC root, and a root list living apart from the fields is a use-after-free
# waiting for a page to allocate enough. GatherSuspensionRoots sits next to the
# struct and walks all of it.
#
# The header grew by more, because Suspension is written out where it lives:
# what a suspended call consists of is the substance of async/await, and the
# list is the same one the handler table records depths of.
#
# Grew again for generators, and by lines rather than by members or methods --
# which is the shape a well-factored addition has here. The member count did not
# move at all: a generator's state lives beside its object on the heap, next to
# the compiled patterns and the weak tables, so nothing new is held here. What
# grew is description -- the two halves a suspend was split into, and what each
# of `yield`, `next`, `throw` and `return` does to a filed frame.
budget: Interpreter header_lines=530 public_methods=44 members=16
# The machine's state, grouped. Five vectors and nothing else: it is a bag of
# stacks by design, and anything that wants to be a *method* on it belongs on
# Interpreter, next to the property lookups the opcodes call.
# Grew by one for the locals stack: the bindings of every frame whose scopes
# nothing can capture, one contiguous slice per frame. A sixth stack rather
# than a vector per frame, for the reason the other five are shared -- a vector
# per frame is a heap allocation per call, which is the cost this whole change
# exists to remove. It is a GC root like the rest, and GatherVmRoots walks it.
budget: VmState     header_lines=36 public_methods=0  members=6
